A||||3||mblackwell||Non-Fiero help needed, very important.||||1||
Z||000000||mblackwell||04-14-2001||03:49 PM||||My mom has an '89 Crown Victoria LTD 5.0 liter.  The fan clutch broke and the fan blades destroyed the radiator.  I got a junk yard radiator and am installing it now. The radiator has 2 rubber hoses and two small metal tubes that attach to it.  I don't know anything about cars so I don't know what the metal tubes are for.  Anyway, I couldn't get the lower one out, the metal ring on the radiator turned with it.  I got the radiator out, but that metal ring is stuck on the end of the tube.  My question: would it be ok to leave that tube off and cap off the hole on the new radiator.  And would it be ok to drive it for a week like that?<P>Thanks a lot. Matt ||||reg||1
Z||000001||drednot||04-14-2001||04:04 PM||||If you don't attach the hoses, the car will drive about a block before the transmission looses all of it's fluid.  Those hoses are for the automatic transmission cooler.  They are under about 120 psi when the car is in gear.  They must be clean and connected.  after you get the hoses hooked up, it would probably be a wise idea to change the automatic transmission fluid and filter.  If you don't know how, any shop will and it should not cost more than $39.99.||||reg||11
Z||000002||mblackwell||04-14-2001||04:07 PM||||Ok, thanks. I'll have to figure out a way to get a grip on that thing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||drednot||04-14-2001||04:12 PM||||Clarification-- In all cars with Automatic Transmissions the radiator has 2 hoses and 2 metal lines running in to it.  The 2 big rubber hoses are for coolant (16 psi)  The 2 metal lines are for the automatic transmission.  All automatic transmissions generate an extreme amount of heat.  They need some way to get rid if it or they will boil over.  that is what those 2 metal lines are for.  they are very high pressure.||||reg||12
